WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.283 --> 00:00:01.116
(audience clapping)

00:00:01.116 --> 00:00:01.955
- Let's close out with something wild

00:00:01.955 --> 00:00:04.208
we came across on social media.

00:00:04.208 --> 00:00:05.363
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:05.363 --> 00:00:08.150
(rock music)
(audience clapping)

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.100
Artists use so many things
to create their works.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.310
Paint, charcoals, clay,
even reclaimed objects.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.210
But have you ever seen this?

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.800
'Cause I've never seen this.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.633
That's Michael.

00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:19.680
And he patiently uses the sun

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.000
and a magnifying glass to burn
images into a wooden canvas.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.260
That's insane to me.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.990
Check out some of his finished pieces.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.923
What?

00:00:30.223 --> 00:00:32.400
That's so insane to me.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.990
We have Michael on the line right now

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.150
watching on Idaho News 6.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.290
What's up, man?

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.805
You are so gifted.

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.411
(audience cheering)

00:00:40.411 --> 00:00:42.420
- Hey Kelly, how's it going?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.570
- I mean, first of all, you
have like the patience of Job.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.280
I'm like, that has to take some time.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.900
How did you come up with this?

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.790
- Well, I've been an
artist for many years.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.650
I went to college for fine arts,

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.160
but it wasn't until 2012
that I packed my bag

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.220
and I went on a long journey
from South Korea to Greece.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.350
And I knew that I wanted
to travel and create art,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.330
so I needed to find a tool

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.950
that I could fit in my pack

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.170
without taking up too much weight.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.270
And one day, I think it
was the day that I started

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.650
to realize I was losing my hair.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.830
I felt the sun so beating
down on my forehead

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:17.220
that I knew I could use the
sun as a source of inspiration

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.870
or as the tool to paint.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.580
So, I never looked back.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.040
And it was at that point that
I picked up a magnifying glass

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.389
and I started to create artwork.

00:01:25.389 --> 00:01:29.130
Some of 'em take anywhere
from one to 40 hours.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:32.160
Oftentimes I'm standing
in direct sunlight.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.320
Right now, it's 12 degrees
out in Boise, Idaho,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.600
but it's sunny, so I could
technically walk outside

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.580
and create art, but it's freezing.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.970
I could also do it in the
summertime too, when it's 120 out.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.920
And as long as the sun's shining,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.050
I can create incredible pieces of art,

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.910
like the ones you see behind me.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.890
And I also get an
incredible workout from it.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.160
It's something that mixes the beauty

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.560
of exercise and creativity.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.393
- It's amazing.

00:01:56.393 --> 00:01:57.570
What's your teaching method?

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.310
You teach people now to do this, right?

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.140
- I teach people, but I wanna preface

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.700
everything I'm about to say.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:03.870
Do not try this at home.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.010
I'm a skilled professional.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.350
I've been doing this for years.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.400
I've been teaching this
technique since about 2013.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.290
And I used to go to parks and
the beach on my free time,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.274
and I would set up,

00:02:17.274 --> 00:02:20.670
I'd set up a piece of artwork
and I'd start working.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.520
And behind me, I would have a crowd

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.570
of 30 to 40 people gathering,
asking me how I did this.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:28.770
So I said here,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.330
and I'd pull out a magnifying glass,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.890
a pair of protective eyewear,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.620
and a hat, which I provided for everybody.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.340
And I taught them how they too
can create art with the sun.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.340
And so, you know, despite the patience,

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.550
I think the most fundamental
part of the whole thing

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.880
is it teaches and inspires children.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.050
The magic behind it.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.390
The magic behind the power of the sun,

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.470
which it's truth and it's
real and it's guidance

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.990
and it's patience and it's respect.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:02.520
And for children, to see
the gleam in their eye

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.800
that what they're witnessing is magic.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.410
And for me to tell them,
"Hey, this is truth."

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.988
It's not a rabbit in a hat trick.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:09.821
- Yeah.

00:03:09.821 --> 00:03:12.750
- This is the power of
the sun in your hand.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.040
- That is so magical.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.490
You just made it way cooler
just with that explanation,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.020
you should write a children's book.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.070
I was like, what?

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.360
Thank you so much, Michael.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:22.890
This is so inspiring.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.440
Like what you do, it's so
different, creatively speaking,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.080
and what dedication it
takes is incredible.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.964
I'm liking your post right now, all right.

00:03:29.964 --> 00:03:31.340
- [Michael] Thank you so much, Kelly,

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.320
for having me on your show.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.153
- Absolutely.

00:03:33.153 --> 00:03:34.590
Thank you for creating such beautiful art.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.820
I mean, that's not all, my man.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.380
Our season long partner, Pilot Pen,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.870
makers of Precise the go-to pen
of "The Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.150
want to honor truly unstoppable artists

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.080
just like yourself, sir.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.480
So, they're gonna be writing
you a check for $1,000.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.680
How's that sound?

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.708
- No way.

00:03:47.708 --> 00:03:49.587
Awesome, thank you.

00:03:49.587 --> 00:03:51.305
- Thank you so much, Michael.

00:03:51.305 --> 00:03:53.040
Have such a great 2024.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.423
Thank you to all our guests this hour.

00:03:54.423 --> 00:03:58.350
Sophia Vergara, Theresa
Caputo, and Say She She.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.950
Have a great day, y'all,
and if it's not, change it.

